    "ls|LS16_S14_U_95": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF INFORMATION AND BROADCASTING \n \nLOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION No. 95 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 08/02/2018  \n  \nVIOLATION OF NORMS BY TV CHANNELS \n \n*95.  SHRI M. I. SHANAVAS: \n  \nWill the Minister of INFORMATION AND BROADCASTING   \nbe pleased to state: \n \n(a) whether a number of television channels have reportedly been violating the norms \nlaid down for telecasting the programmes/ advertisements on their channels in the \nrecent past;  \n(b) if so, the details thereof; and \n(c) the names of the televisi on channels which have been found to be violating the \nnorms for the purpose? \n \nANSWER \n \nTHE MINISTER OF INFORMATION & BROADCASTING \n(SMT. SMRITI ZUBIN IRANI) \n \n \n (a) to (c):  A Statement is laid on the Table of the House. \n******** \n\nSTATEMENT AS REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PARTS  (a) TO ( c) OF LOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION NO. 95 FOR ANSWER ON 08.02.2018 \n \n(a) to (c)    All programmes and advertisements telecast on private satellite TV channels \nand transmitted /re-transmitted through the Cable TV network , DTH platform and  HITS \nare required to adhere to the Programme Code and Advertising Code prescribed unde r \nthe Cable Television Netwo rks (Regulation) Act, 1995 and Cable Television Network \nRules, 1994 framed thereunder. The Act does not provide for pre -censorship of any \nprogramme or advertisement telecast on such TV channels. However, all programmes \nand advertisements are required to be in conformity with the  said Programme and \nAdvertising Codes which contain a whole range of parameters to regulate programmes \nand advertiseme nts on TV channels. Action is taken against TV channels  as per \nUplinking and Downlinking Guidelines  whenever any  violation of the aforesaid \nProgramme and Advertising Codes is established.   Names of the channels found to \nhave violated the Programme and Advertising Codes is Annexed. \n \n********* \n\nANNEXURE \n \nANNEXURE AS REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PARTS (a) to (c) OF LOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION No. 95  FOR  ANSWER ON 08.02.2018 \n \nName of the Private satellite TV channels which have violated the Programme and \nAdvertising codes during the last three years and current year \n \nYear – 2014 \n \nSl. \nNo. \nName of the \nchannels \nSl. No. Name of the \nchannels \nSl. No. Name of the \nchannels \n1. WB 8. Amrita TV 15. Naxatra News  \n2. Lemon News 9. DY 365 16. Headlines Today \n3. Bansal News 10. Sony TV 17. Sudarshan TV \n4. NE TV 11. Vasanth TV   \n5. Zee TV 12. News Live TV   \n6. India News 13. TV-5  TV   \n7. Color 14. Kalaignar TV   \n \nYear – 2015 \n \nSl. \nNo. \nName of the \nchannels \nSl. No. Name of the \nchannels \nSl. No. Name of the \nchannels \n1. Colors TV 8. Jai Hind 15. Channel 2 News \n2. Lemon News 9. Aljazeera TV 16. A2Z News TV \n3. NTV  10. Sathiyam TV 17. DY 365 \n4. 24 Ghanta TV 11. Sudarshan  News    \n5. NTV 12. Raj News   \n6. Satlon News 13. Raj News   \n7. TV9 14. Russia Today TV   \n \nYear- 2016 \n \nSl. \nNo. \nName of the \nchannels \nSl. No. Name of the \nchannels \nSl. No. Name of the \nchannels \n1. Care World 8. Oscar Movies 15. MBC TV \n2. F TV 9. Aaj Tak 16. Reporter TV \n3. Raj News 10. ABP News   \n4. CVR English News \nChannel \n11. NDTV INDIA   \n5. Sathiyam TV 12. Care World   \n6. NDTV India TV 13. News Time Assam   \n7. NDTV 24x7 14. People TV   \n \nYear- 2017 \n \nSl. \nNo. \nName of the channels \n1. TV-9 (Marathi) \n2. V TV channel \n3. DY 365 TV channel \n4. TV-9 (Marathi) \n \nYear - 2018 \nSl. No. Name of the channels \n----NIL--- \n********** ",
    "ls|LS16_S14_U_96": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF INFORMATION & BROADCASTING \n \nLOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION NO. 96 \n(TO BE ANSWERED ON 08.2.2018) \n  \nCASUAL WORKERS IN AIR/DD KENDRAS  \n \n*96. SHRI RAJU SHETTY: \n \n \nWill the MINISTER OF INFORMATION AND BROADCASTING \nbe pleased to state: \n \n(a) the number of casual announcers, typists and librarians working in All India Radio and \nDoordarshan Kendras along with the reasons for hiring such casual workers; \n(b) whether the Government proposes to regularise the services of such workers keeping in view \nthe long services rendered by them; and \n(c) if so, the details thereof? \nANSWER \nTHE MINISTER OF INFORMATION AND BROADCASTING                                     \n(SMT. SMRITI ZUBIN IRANI) \n \n (a)  to (c):  A statement is laid on the Table of the House. \n \n******* \n \n \n  \n\nSTATEMENT REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PARTS (a) TO (c) OF LOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION NO. 96 FOR ANSWER ON 08.2.2018. \n(a): Prasar Bharati has informed that: \nCasual Announcers/Presenters are not regul ar employees of All India Radio (AIR) and \nas such data on numbers is not maintained centrally. Rather they are empanelled \nperiodically based on the suitability of their voices and are called to perform as and \nwhen required by AIR Stations. The number of su ch empanelled Announcers/Presenters \nvaries from station/channel to station/channel as they are empanelled as per the \nexigencies of the station/channel. Approximate number of casual Assignees on the panel \nacross the network is about 1500. \nAs far as Casual A nnouncers/Presenters are concerned, they are booked for an \nassignment as per day to day requirement of programmes at AIR Stations. The number \nof such bookings is limited to six days only in a month and 72 days in a year. Their \ncontinuance on the panel is d ecided by the programme exigencies and performance of \nthe artists.  \nAs per available record, no casual Librarian and Typists are working in AIR Stations. \nIn so far as Doordarshan is concerned, no Casual Announcers, Typists and Librarian \nhave been engaged in Doordarshan. \n \n(b): Prasar Bharati has further informed that at present there is no Scheme for their \nregularization.  \n(c): Does not arise. \n***** \n ",
    "ls|LS16_S14_U_97": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIAMINISTRY OF RURAL DEVELOPMENTDEPARTMENT OF RURAL DEVELOPMENTLOK SABHASTARRED QUESTION NO. 97TO BE ANSWERED ON 08.02.2018PRADHAN MANTRI GRAM SADAK YOJANA*97. SHRI JANARDAN SINGH SIGRIWAL: SHRI RAM TAHAL CHOUDHARY:Will the Minister of RURAL DEVELOPMENT be pleased to state:(a) the number of villages covered under the Pradhan Mantri Gram Sadak Yojana (PMGSY) and the number of those yet to be connected by roads, State/UTwise; (b) the total length of roads constructed under various phases of the scheme during the Twelfth Five Year Plan period and 2017-18, State/UT and year-wise; (c) the details of the funds allocated, sanctioned, released and the expenditure incurred under various phases of PMGSY during the said period, State/UT-wise and year-wise; (d) whether the work on certain projects under the scheme is running behind the schedule; and (e) if so, the details thereof and the reasons therefor, State/UT-wise?ANSWERMINISTER OF RURAL DEVELOPMENT(SHRI NARENDRA SINGH TOMAR)(a) to (e): A Statement is laid on the Table of the House.*****\n\nStatement referred to in reply to Parts (a) to (e) of Lok Sabha Starred Question No. 97  for answer on 08.02.2018(a):Rural Roads’ is a State subject and Pradhan Mantri Gram Sadak Yojana (PMGSY) is a one-time special intervention of Government of India to provide rural connectivity, by way of a single all-weather road, to the eligible unconnected habitations in the core network with a population of 500 persons and above (Census 2001) in plain areas and 250 and above in Special Category States. As per PMGSY guidelines, the unit of the programme is habitation and    not a Village. The number of eligible unconnected habitations as per the Core Network under PMGSY is 1,78,184, out of which the Ministry has sanctioned 1, 64,547 habitations till 31st December, 2017. The States have reported to have connected 1,30,974 habitations by all-weather road till 31st December,2017. Further, the States have completed 14,620 habitations, which are a part of core network of PMGSY, through State Sponsored Schemes bringing total connectivity to 1,45,594 habitations.  (b): State/UT and year-wise details of the total length of roads constructed under various phases of the scheme during the Twelfth Five Year Plan period and 2017-18 (till 31st December, 2017) is given at Annexure –I.(c): Under PMGSY, the allocation/release of funds to the States is based on the execution capacity and the works in hand of the respective States. During 12th five year plan, total allocation for PMGSY (Revised Estimate) was Rs. 73,126 crore. For the current year, allocation at revised estimate is Rs. 16,900 crore. State/UT-wise and year wise details of funds released and the expenditure incurred under various phases of PMGSY during Twelfth Five Year Plan period and 2017-18 (till 31st January, 2018) is given at  Annexure – II.(d) to (e):As per the programme guidelines, the relevant projects are required to be executed and completed within 9 working months. In case, the period for execution is likely to be adversely affected by monsoon or other seasonal factors, the time period for execution would be 12 calendar months. Where a package comprises more than one roadwork, the total time given for completion of the package shall not exceed 12 months. Time limit upto 18 calendar months is allowed for completion of State-I work of hill roads. However, certain projects got delayed for completion for various reasons.Reasons for the delay in the implementation of PMGSY in some of the States as reported by them, inter-alia, include: i)Inadequate execution and contracting capacity;ii)Difficult terrain particularly in Hill States and North-Eastern States; shorter working season;iii)Scarcity of the construction materials.Out of 1,46,145 works sanctioned prior to 2015-16 (more than 2 years old), 1,38, 439 works have been completed. The State/UT wise details of works sanctioned and completed is given at Annexure-III.The Government of India has taken number of initiatives to accelerate execution of PMGSY to substantially complete the habitation connectivity as per the Core Network of PMGSY by March,2019.  \n\nAnnexure - IAnnexure referred to in point (b) of Lok Sabha Starred Q.No. 97  for answer on 8.2.2018Total length of the Roads completed under varios phases of PMGSY during the 12th Five year Plan period and 2017-18(Length in Km)State(s)Length completed during 2012-13Length completed during 2013-14Length completed during 2014-15Length completed during 2015-16Length completed during 2016-17Length completed during 12th Five year PlanLength completed during 2017-18 upto Dec'20171234567891Andhra Pradesh  400.35191.58595.13849.73733.552770.35120.312Arunachal Pradesh393.67489.04546.58512.671360.513302.46691.423Assam 1456.16957.96869.81477.63929.524691.08404.174Bihar (RWD) 4240.262636.603414.793682.796601.6220576.062297.045Bihar (NEA)2101.37527.26217.1347.300.002893.050.006Chhattisgarh 1024.081292.052648.141859.481019.577843.33508.567Goa 0.000.000.000.000.000.000.008Gujarat180.47896.291892.161670.68211.884851.4828.449Haryana 69.263.28633.39318.9962.851087.7738.4910Himachal Pradesh 0.00134.49484.96755.311429.272804.031341.1811Jammu & Kashmir 1411.10891.79934.66488.111785.165510.821032.5712Jharkhand 1236.741030.731750.321346.243119.528483.552245.1613Karnataka 386.02211.43627.68831.23897.092953.4544.9314Kerala 108.71192.46345.74363.94314.331325.18153.0915Madhya Pradesh 2754.183006.275180.924453.235081.9720476.573261.5616Maharashtra 649.54448.88499.97892.132000.704491.22286.6217Manipur 424.48533.12300.01543.821485.853287.28340.6118Meghalaya 22.7723.6844.59193.29368.87653.2075.5719Mizoram 93.2077.2848.60136.54298.08653.7043.3420Nagaland 93.50293.20215.30129.00395.001126.0062.0021Odisha2401.263063.223842.683779.985796.9318884.073362.8822Punjab325.54730.38737.46545.00586.532924.91723.22\n\n23Rajasthan2140.002290.313233.332420.753110.1013194.492118.5524Sikkim 48.4499.36120.92344.50247.42860.6